subsection.p:
          The following code shows the typical usage for a controller, and how to do the bidding.

Controller myController = new MyController();
myController.setView(new MyView());
myController.setModel(new MyModel());


          Controllers have three responsibilities: 


Set the model and the view (usually in the constructor)

public LoginController() { 
    setModel(new LoginModel()); 
    setView(new LoginView()); 
} 

Handle controls in the doHandleControl method

protected void doHandleControl(Control inControl) throws ControlException { 
  if (inControl.matchesID(VALIDATE_LOGIN_CONTROL_ID)) { 
    doValidateLogin(); 
  } 
} 
              Controllers respond to Control objects from multiple sources, including: 


Issued from Views as a result of user interaction via an issueControl() command 

Issued from child Sub-Controllers in the chain of responsibility using a handleControl() command

Issued locally using the handleControl() command



Startup and shutdown

            startup() executes any actions necessary to begin operation of this controller/model/view cluster: 

public void startup() { 
    LoginView myView = (LoginView)getView(); 
    myView.setViewBounds(myView.CENTRED); 
    showView(myView); 
}
             shutdown() executes any actions necessary to end operation of the controller/model/view cluster and cleanup the resources.

public void stutdown() { 
    databaseConnection.release();
    super.shutdown();
}
             The startup() and the shutdown() method are never called by the Scope framework, the user has the responsability to call those methods.


          The following code is a typical implementation for a controller and a child controller. 

          The child controller will handle the processing of controls it doesn't know to its parent 
          controller, thus this hierarchy of controllers forms a Chain of responsability.

public class ParentController extends BasicController {
	public ParentController() {
		ParentView view = new ParentView();
		setModel(new ParentModel());
		setView(view);
		ChildController childController = new ChildController(view.getChildView());
		this.addChild(childController);
	}
}
public class ChildController extends BasicController {
	public ChildController(ChildView view) {
		setModel(new ChildModel());
		setView(view);
	}
}
